With die 1 = 0 and die 3 = 9, the settlement is 9 + die 2, where die 2 has mean 2.5. So the public fair value is 11.5, and the settlement takes values 9, 10, 11, 13 or 18.

I'm short 4 and the ask is pulled, so I can only buy back at my bid. An informed seller sells to me only when the settlement is below my bid, which loses money on that trade. The lowest possible settlement is 9, so any bid at or below 8.9 has no adverse-selection cost. Against noise sellers, the expected gain from a bid b is proportional to P(V < b) × (11.5 − b), which peaks near b ≈ 7. A bid of 7.0 gives the best expected value.

