With die 3 = 7 revealed, the fair value is 7 + E[die 1] + E[die 2] = 7 + 5.75 + 5 = 17.75. We're short 4, so only buying back is allowed. Buying one unit at bid b earns (V − b) at settlement.

A noise trader sells to us when their valuation is below b, which is about Φ((b − 17.75)/6.6), and that trade is worth 17.75 − b on average. An informed trader sells exactly when the settlement value V is below b, which is adverse selection. Balancing these, the marginal gain from raising the bid is roughly zero near b ≈ 12.5. That's well below fair value, which is appropriate given the adverse selection from informed sellers.

