The coin must be the fair one: a bias-1 coin cannot produce the tail on flip 2. Flips 5 and 6 are therefore independent fair flips, and the public fair value of the settlement is 1 + 0.5 + 0.5 = 2.0.

The settlement is one of 1, 2, or 3 heads, so an informed counterparty only trades when the value falls outside our quotes. A tight spread around 2 loses money to informed flow, because informed trades at value 1 or 3 cost us about 1 per unit. Quoting bid 0.7 and ask 3.3 means no informed trader can profitably trade, and noise traders still give us a positive expected edge.

