Both revealed flips are tails, so the always-heads coin is ruled out (it would have produced heads). The coin is fair. Flips 3 to 6 are independent 50/50, so the public fair value is 2 (flips 1 and 2 contribute 0, and the remaining four flips contribute 2 in expectation).

Against a fair value of 2, the adverse-selection cost of an informed buyer grows quickly as the ask moves up. The expected edge from noise-trader flow, (ask − 2) × P(noise buys), peaks near an ask of about 3.3 to 3.4, where the informed-buyer loss is small. On the bid side, a bid of 0.6 keeps a positive edge against noise sellers while losing little to informed sellers, and the bid rounds down cleanly. I'm slightly long, so I'm keeping the bid conservative.

{"bid": 0.6, "ask": 3.4}

Flips 1 and 2 were both tails. A coin with P(heads)=1 can't produce tails, so the coin must be fair. The remaining four flips are each heads with probability 0.5, so the public fair value is 2.

Informed traders trade only when the settlement value is on the favourable side of my quote, so they are the adverse-selection risk. Noise traders' valuations don't depend on the settlement, so their trades earn the spread relative to 2. Taking the expected profit of each side as a function of price, the bid and ask each come out near 0.17 per quote around 2 at about ±1.4, so I'll quote 0.6 and 3.4.

